" (https://arxiv.org/pdf/1205.2926)\nciteturn9view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 9; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1205.2926\",\"lineno\":150}); Total lines: 369\nL45@P1: It is well known that the fast Fourier transform (FFT) can be used to\nL46@P1: compute the NTT using O(Llog L) operations in Fp. For completeness, a\nL47@P1: simple in-place iterative radix-2 FFT algorithm is shown in Algorithm 1.\nL48@P1: Algorithm 1: Simple FFT\nL49@P1: Input: ω ∈ Fp and x = (x0, . . . , xL−1) ∈ (Fp)\nL50@P1: L, with L = 2ℓ\nL51@P1: Output: DFT of x with respect to ω, in bit-reversed order\nL52@P1: 1 for i ← 1, 2, . . . , ℓ do\nL53@P1: 2 ζ ← ω\nL54@P1: 2\nL55@P1: i−1\nL56@P1: 3 m ← 2\nL57@P1: ℓ−i\nL58@P1: 4 for 0 ≤ j < 2\nL59@P1: i−1 do\nL60@P1: 5 t ← 2jm\nL61@P1: 6 for 0 ≤ k < m do\nL62@P1: 7\nL63@P1: \u0014\nL64@P1: xt+k\nL65@P1: xt+k+m\nL66@P1: \u0015\nL67@P1: ←\nL68@P1: \u0014\nL69@P1: xt+k + xt+k+m\nL70@P1: ζ\nL71@P1: k\nL72@P1: (xt+k − xt+k+m)\nL73@P1: \u0015\nL74@P1: 8 end\nL75@P1: 9 end\nL76@P1: 10 end\nL77@P1: Our main focus in this paper is on the butterfly operation in line 7, which\nL78@P1: computes (X, Y ) 7→ (X +Y, W(X −Y )) for some fixed root of unity W ∈ Fp\nL79@P1: (a suitable power of ω). At first glance this requires one modular addition,\nL80@P1: one modular subtraction, and one modular multiplication, and this is how\nL81@P1: the butterfly is usually implemented. Of course these modular operations\nL82@P1: are themselves implemented on modern microprocessors using more basic\nL83@P1: primitives. For example, a modular addition is usually implemented as an\nL84@P1: ordinary integer addition, followed by a comparison with p, followed by a\nL85@P1: conditional subtraction. In this paper we investigate the butterfly at this\nL86@P1: lower level, showing how to streamline the implementation to reduce the\nL87@P1: number of comparisons and conditional operations. Another interpretation\nL88@P1: is that we have reduced the number of modular reductions.\nL89@P1: 2. A typical butterfly implementation\nL90@P1: Victor Shoup’s NTL (Number Theory Library) [6] is a popular C++ li\u0002brary used in computational number theory. It makes heavy use of the NTT.\nL91@P1: Its implementation of the butterfly (X, Y ) 7→ (X + Y, W(X − Y )) may be\nL92@P1: expressed by the pseudocode shown in Algorithm 2. It has been simplified to\nL93@P1: focus attention on the arithmetic aspects, ignoring issues like loop unrolling,\nL94@P1-2: software pipelining, and locality. All variables represent register-sized quan\u0002tities.FASTER ARITHMETIC FOR NUMBER-THEORETIC TRANSFORMS 3\nL95@P2: Algorithm 2: NTL butterfly implementation\nL96@P2: Input: p < β/2\nL97@P2: 0 < W < p\nL98@P2: W′ = ⌊W β/p⌋, 0 < W′ < β\nL99@P2: 0 ≤ X < p\nL100@P2: 0 ≤ Y < p\nL101@P2: Output: X′ = X + Y (mod p)\nL102@P2: Y\nL103@P2: ′ = W(X − Y ) (mod p)\nL104@P2: 1 X′ ← X + Y\nL105@P2: 2 if X′ ≥ p then X′ ← X′ − p\nL106@P2: 3 T ← X − Y\nL107@P2: 4 if T < 0 then T ← T + p\nL108@P2: 5 Q ← ⌊W′T /β⌋\nL109@P2: 6 Y\nL110@P2: ′ ← (W T − Qp) mod β\nL111@P2: 7 if Y\nL112@P2: ′ ≥ p then Y′ ← Y′ − p\nL113@P2: 8 return X′, Y ′\nL114@P2: Note that W and W′ are independent of the data being transformed; they\nL115@P2: can be precomputed and reused for each transform. A similar comment\nL116@P2: applies to all the butterfly algorithms considered in this paper.\nL117@P2: Theorem 1. Algorithm 2 is correct.\nL118@P2: Proof. Lines 1–2 compute the sum X + Y and reduce it modulo p to the\nL119@P2: standard interval [0, p), using the assumption p < β/2 to avoid overflow in\nL120@P2: the first line. Lines 3–4 compute T = X − Y (mod p) in the same way,\nL121@P2: assuming that T has a signed type for the comparison.\nL122@P2: Lines 5–7 compute the product W T (mod p). Line 5 first generates an\nL123@P2: estimated quotient Q. By the definition of W′ and Q we have\nL124@P2: 0 ≤\nL125@P2: W β\nL126@P2: p\nL127@P2: − W′ < 1, 0 ≤\nL128@P2: W′T\nL129@P2: β\nL130@P2: − Q < 1.\nL131@P2: Multiplying by T p/β and p respectively, and adding, yields\nL132@P2: 0 ≤ W T − Qp < T p\nL133@P2: β\nL134@P2: + p < 2p < β.\nL135@P2: In particular, line 6 correctly computes Y\nL136@P2: ′ = W T − Qp, and the single\nL137@P2: correction in line 7 suffices to reduce it into [0, p). \u0003\nL138@P2: The operations performed by Algorithm 2 map comfortably onto modern\nL139@P2: instruction sets. In line 5, the expression ⌊W′T /β⌋ represents the high word\nL140@P2: of the product W′T, which can typically be obtained by a single hardware\nL141@P2: multiply instruction. Both W T and Qp in line 6 require only the low word\nL142@P2-3: of the product. The conditional additions and subtractions in lines 2, 44 DAVID HARVEY\nL143@P3: and 7 are simple enough that a modern compiler will implement them by a\nL144@P3: conditional move instruction rather than by a branch.\nL145@P3: If we assume that X and Y are distributed uniformly in [0, p), then the\nL146@P3: first two conditions (lines 2 and 4) hold with probability 50%. The behaviour\nL147@P3: of the third condition (line 7) is more complex. If the quantity W β/p − W′\nL148@P3: considered in the proof of the theorem happens to be close to zero, then the\nL149@P3: condition will be satisfied with very low probability. At the other extreme,\nL150@P3: if W β/p−W′is close to unity, and if we assume that p is near β/2, then one\nL151@P3: can show the condition is satisfied with probability about 25%. In this case\nL152@P3: it is still reasonable to prefer a conditional move instruction to a branch.\nL153@P3: The modular multiplication algorithm in lines 5–7 is attributed to Shoup\nL154@P3: in [2], but does not seem to have been published. It first appears in NTL\nL155@P3: version 5.4 in 2005. The use of a suitable precomputed approximation to\nL156@P3: W/p implies that only a single correction step (line 7) is necessary, and that\nL157@P3: the remainder is obtained using only multiplication modulo β (line 6), an\nL158@P3: advantage on processors that can compute the low word faster than the full\nL159@P3: product. The latter idea is also used heavily in the division algorithm of [4].\nL160@P3: In our exposition we assumed for simplicity that p < β/2, but Niels M¨oller\nL161@P3: has pointed out (personal communication) that this can be improved. The\nL162@P3: modular subtraction can be made to work for any p < β by replacing the\nL163@P3: condition T < 0 by X < Y , or indeed by using the borrow generated by the\nL164@P3: subtraction T = X − Y . The addition can be treated similarly by rewriting\nL165@P3: it as X′ = X − (p − Y ). A more careful analysis of the candidate remainder\nL166@P3: W T −Qp then shows that the entire algorithm works for any p < β/φ where\nL167@P3: φ =\nL168@P3: 1\nL169@P3: 2\nL170@P3: (1 + √\nL171@P3: 5) ≈ 1.618.\nL172@P3: 3. A modified butterfly\nL173@P3: In this section we propose several modifications to Algorithm 2. Our\nL174@P3: motivation is that the adjustment steps in lines 2, 4 and 7 are relatively\nL175@P3: expensive on modern microprocessors, compared to hardware integer multi\u0002pliers, which in recent years have become very fast.\nL176@P3: Our first observation is that Shoup’s algorithm for computing W T (mod p)\nL177@P3: does not require that 0 ≤ T < p; in fact the proof given above shows that\nL178@P3: it works for any 0 ≤ T < β. Therefore we may replace lines 3–4 by simply\nL179@P3: T ← X − Y + p, after which we have 0 ≤ T < 2p < β, and the algorithm\nL180@P3: proceeds as before. This simplification is not new, although it does not ap\u0002pear to be well known. It is not used in NTL. It was apparently used by\nL181@P3: Fabrice Bellard in a computation of π to 2.7 trillion decimal places in 2009\nL182@P3: (personal communication).\nL183@P3: Our second observation appears to be new, and is the main novelty intro\u0002duced in the present paper. Namely, we may also remove the adjustment in\nL184@P3: line 7, provided that throughout the FFT we use a redundant representa\u0002tion for elements of Fp. That is, instead of representing elements of Fp by\nL185@P3-4: integers in [0, p), we use the wider interval [0, 2p), so each element has twoFASTER ARITHMETIC FOR NUMBER-THEORETIC TRANSFORMS 5\nL186@P4: possible representations. For this to work, we must modify the butterfly to\nL187@P4: accept inputs in [0, 2p), and we must impose the stronger condition p < β/4.\nL188@P4: Pseudocode for the resulting butterfly is shown in Algorithm 3.\nL189@P4: According to Jason Papadopolous (personal communication), around 1998\nL190@P4: Ernst Mayer suggested the use of a redundant representation in the context\nL191@P4: of a fast Galois transform, i.e. a Fourier transform over Fq\nL192@P4: 2 where q = 261−1.\nL193@P4: Our new algorithm may be regarded as a generalisation of this idea to the\nL194@P4: case of an NTT with arbitrary modulus p.\nL195@P4: Algorithm 3: Modified Shoup butterfly\nL196@P4: Input: p < β/4\nL197@P4: 0 < W < p\nL198@P4: W′ = ⌊W β/p⌋, 0 < W′ < β\nL199@P4: 0 ≤ X < 2p\nL200@P4: 0 ≤ Y < 2p\nL201@P4: Output: X′ = X + Y (mod p), 0 ≤ X′ < 2p\nL202@P4: Y\nL203@P4: ′ = W(X − Y ) (mod p), 0 ≤ Y′ < 2p\nL204@P4: 1 X′ ← X + Y\nL205@P4: 2 if X′ ≥ 2p then X′ ← X′ − 2p\nL206@P4: 3 T ← X − Y + 2p\nL207@P4: 4 Q ← ⌊W′T /β⌋\nL208@P4: 5 Y\nL209@P4: ′ ← (W T − Qp) mod β\nL210@P4: 6 return X′, Y ′\nL211@P4: Theorem 2. Algorithm 3 is correct.\nL212@P4: Proof. Lines 1–2 compute a representative for X +Y (mod p) in the interval\nL213@P4: [0, 2p); this does not overflow as p < β/4. Line 3 computes a representative T\nL214@P4: for X−Y (mod p) in the interval [0, 4p). Lines 4–5 compute a representative\nL215@P4: for W T (mod p) in the interval [0, 2p), in the same way as in the proof of\nL216@P4: Theorem 1. \u0003\nL217@P4: Both outputs X′, Y\nL218@P4: ′\nL219@P4: lie in [0, 2p), ready to be processed by a subsequent\nL220@P4: butterfly. Depending on the needs of the NTT user, it may be necessary to\nL221@P4: normalise the final NTT output into the interval [0, p), imposing an addi\u0002tional O(L) cost.\nL222@P4: 4. Variants\nL223@P4: In some situations one requires a butterfly of the form (X, Y ) 7→ (X +\nL224@P4: W Y, X−W Y ). This appears if one switches from a ‘decimation-in-frequency’\nL225@P4: transform to a ‘decimation-in-time’ transform. It also appears naturally in\nL226@P4: the inverse FFT obtained by running Algorithm 1 backwards. Algorithm\nL227@P4-5: 4 shows an analogue of Algorithm 3 for this case. The main difference is6 DAVID HARVEY\nL228@P5: that elements of Fp are represented by an integer in [0, 4p), so each residue\nL229@P5: has four possible representatives. As in Algorithm 3, this strategy saves two\nL230@P5: modular reductions compared to the usual implementation.\nL231@P5: Algorithm 4: Modified inverse butterfly\nL232@P5: Input: p < β/4\nL233@P5: 0 < W < p\nL234@P5: W′ = ⌊W β/p⌋, 0 < W′ < β\nL235@P5: 0 ≤ X < 4p\nL236@P5: 0 ≤ Y < 4p\nL237@P5: Output: X′ = X + W Y (mod p), 0 ≤ X′ < 4p\nL238@P5: Y\nL239@P5: ′ = X − W Y (mod p), 0 ≤ Y′ < 4p\nL240@P5: 1 if X ≥ 2p then X ← X − 2p\nL241@P5: 2 Q ← ⌊W′Y /β⌋\nL242@P5: 3 T ← (W Y − Qp) mod β\nL243@P5: 4 X′ ← X + T\nL244@P5: 5 Y\nL245@P5: ′ ← X − T + 2p\nL246@P5: 6 return X′, Y ′\nL247@P5: Theorem 3. Algorithm 4 is correct.\nL248@P5: Proof. Line 1 reduces X into [0, 2p). Lines 2–3 compute T = W Y (mod p)\nL249@P5: in [0, 2p), in the same way as Algorithm 3. The remaining lines complete\nL250@P5: the calculation of X′ and Y\nL251@P5: ′\nL252@P5: in [0, 4p). \u0003\nL253@P5: The same idea may be applied to butterflies using other modular multi\u0002plication algorithms. Algorithm 5 shows a variant using Montgomery mul\u0002tiplication [5]. The idea of Montgomery multiplication is in effect to replace\nL254@P5: the usual Euclidean quotient by a 2-adic quotient. Compared to Algorithm\nL255@P5: 3, this butterfly algorithm has the advantage that only a single value W′\nL256@P5: must be stored in a table for each root of unity (W is not actually used\nL257@P5: in the algorithm). On the other hand, one of the multiplications modulo β\nL258@P5: has been replaced by a full product, which may be more expensive on some\nL259@P5: processors.\nL260@P5: Theorem 4. Algorithm 5 is correct.\nL261@P5: Proof. Lines 1–3 are identical to the corresponding lines in Algorithm 3. Line\nL262@P5: 4 computes the product W′T, placing the low and high words of the result\nL263@P5: in R0 and R1 respectively, so that 0 ≤ R1 < p. Line 5 computes Q = R0/p\nL264@P5: (mod β) (this may be regarded as a 2-adic approximation to the quotient\nL265@P5: W′T /p). Line 6 computes the high word of Qp, so that 0 ≤ H < p. We\nL266@P5: have Qp = R0 (mod β), so Qp = Hβ + R0. Then W′T − Qp = β(R1 − H),\nL267@P5: and thus W T = R1 − H (mod p). This agrees modulo p with the value\nL268@P5: computed for Y\nL269@P5: ′\nL270@P5-6: in line 7, which lies in the interval [0, 2p).FASTER ARITHMETIC FOR NUMBER-THEORETIC TRANSFORMS 7\nL271@P6: Algorithm 5: Modified Montgomery butterfly\nL272@P6: Input: p odd, p < β/4\nL273@P6: 0 < W < p\nL274@P6: W′ = βW (mod p), 0 < W′ < p\nL275@P6: J = p\nL276@P6: −1\nL277@P6: (mod β)\nL278@P6: 0 ≤ X < 2p\nL279@P6: 0 ≤ Y < 2p\nL280@P6: Output: X′ = X + Y (mod p), 0 ≤ X′ < 2p\nL281@P6: Y\nL282@P6: ′ = W(X − Y ) (mod p), 0 ≤ Y′ < 2p\nL283@P6: 1 X′ ← X + Y\nL284@P6: 2 if X′ ≥ 2p then X′ ← X′ − 2p\nL285@P6: 3 T ← X − Y + 2p\nL286@P6: 4 R1β + R0 ← W′T\nL287@P6: 5 Q ← R0J mod β\nL288@P6: 6 H ← ⌊Qp/β⌋\nL289@P6: 7 Y\nL290@P6: ′ ← R1 − H + p\nL291@P6: 8 return X′, Y ′\nL292@P6: (Usually in Montgomery’s algorithm, a further comparison and condi\u0002tional subtraction/addition is performed to normalise the result into [0, p),\nL293@P6: but we have simply skipped that.) \u0003\nL294@P6: 5. Implementation and performance\nL295@P6: The practical benefit (if any) derived from the new algorithms depends\nL296@P6: heavily on the hardware used. To illustrate what may occur in practice, we\nL297@P6: performed some timing experiments on several machines: a 3.06GHz Intel\nL298@P6: Xeon (model X5675, ‘Westmere’ microarchitecture), a 2.6GHz Intel Xeon\nL299@P6: (model E5-2670, ‘Sandy Bridge’ microarchitecture), and an AMD Vishera\nL300@P6: (model FX-8350, ‘Piledriver’ microarchitecture), all modern 64-bit proces\u0002sors.\nL301@P6: We compared a C implementation of a number-theoretic transform in\u0002corporating Algorithm 3, a similar implementation of a transform based on\nL302@P6: Algorithm 2, and the FFT routine in NTL itself (version 5.5.2). The code is\nL303@P6: available from the author’s web site. It is moderately optimised: the inner\nL304@P6: loops are 2-way unrolled, and the last two layers of the FFT have dedicated\nL305@P6: loops. The values of W and W′ are precomputed and stored in a table.\nL306@P6: Everything was compiled using GCC 4.6.3 on the Intel machines, and GCC\nL307@P6: 4.7.2 on the AMD machine, with the -O2 optimisation flag. We also tried\nL308@P6: the -O3 flag; this made no significant difference to the results. Access to\nL309@P6: the high word of the product of two 64-bit integers is obtained using the\nL310@P6: compiler’s built-in support for 128-bit integer types; in NTL this is handled\nL311@P6-7: via inline assembly macros.8 DAVID HARVEY\nL312@P7: We ran transforms of length 211 = 2048. This is long enough to avoid too\nL313@P7: much loop overhead, but short enough that all memory accesses hit the L1\nL314@P7: cache, so we may ignore locality problems. For our code we used a 62-bit\nL315@P7: prime p, and for NTL we used a 50-bit prime. NTL supports moduli of only\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn9view1 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://github.com/nict-sfl/FasterNTT/commits/master/\",\"lineno\":null}); \nFailed to fetch https://github.com/nict-sfl/FasterNTT/commits/master/: Cache miss--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn9view2 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/nict-sfl/FasterNTT/master/LICENSE\",\"lineno\":null}); \nFailed to fetch https://raw.githubusercontent.com/nict-sfl/FasterNTT/master/LICENSE: Cache miss--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn9view3 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://eprint.iacr.org/2026/307.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); \nURL https://eprint.iacr.org/2026/307.pdf is not accessible via this tool.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2402.06093] Formal Verification of the Sumcheck Protocol (https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.06093)\nciteturn9view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.06093\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 172\nL0: 【0†Search】 【1†Submit】 【2†Donate†info.arxiv.org】 【3†Log in】\nL1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · 【4†Advanced search】\nL5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Cryptography and Security\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 8 Feb 2024]\nL9: # Title:Formal Verification of the Sumcheck Protocol\nL10: \nL11: Authors:【5†Azucena Garvía Bosshard】, 【6†Jonathan Bootle】, 【7†Christoph Sprenger】\nL12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Formal Verification of the Sumcheck Protocol, by Azucena Garv\\'ia Bosshard and Jonathan Bootle and Christoph Sprenger\nL14: \nL15: 【8†View PDF】 【9†HTML (experimental)】\nL16: > Abstract:The sumcheck protocol, introduced in 1992, is an interactive proof which is a key component of many probabilistic proof systems in computational complexity theory and cryptography, some of which have been deployed. However, none of these proof systems based on the sumcheck protocol enjoy a formally-verified security analysis.\nL17: In this paper, we make progress in this direction by providing a formally verified security analysis of the sumcheck protocol using the interactive theorem prover Isabelle/HOL. We follow a general and modular approach.\nL18: > First, we give a general formalization of public-coin interactive proofs. We then define a generalized sumcheck protocol for which we axiomatize the underlying mathematical structure and we establish its soundness and completeness. Finally, we prove that these axioms hold for multivariate polynomials, the original setting of the sumcheck protocol.\nL19: Our modular analysis facilitates formal verification of sumcheck instances based on different mathematical structures with little effort, by simply proving that these structures satisfy the axioms. Moreover, the analysis supports the development and formal verification of future cryptographic protocols using the sumcheck protocol as a building block.\nL20: Comments:  | Full version of CSF 2024 paper\nL21: Subjects:  | Cryptography and Security (cs.CR); Logic in Computer Science (cs.LO)\nL22: Cite as:  | 【10†arXiv:2402.06093】 [cs.CR]\nL23:    | (or 【11†arXiv:2402.06093v1】 [cs.CR] for this version)\nL24:    | 【12†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2402.06093†doi.org】 arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL25: ## Submission history\nL26: \nL27: From: Jonathan Bootle [【13†view email】]\nL28: [v1] Thu, 8 Feb 2024 23:01:32 UTC (162 KB)\nL29: \nL30: Full-text links:\nL31: \nL32: ## Access Paper:\nL33: \nL34: View a PDF of the paper titled Formal Verification of the Sumcheck Protocol, by Azucena Garv\\'ia Bosshard and Jonathan Bootle and Christoph Sprenger\nL35: \nL36:   * 【8†View PDF】\nL37:   * 【9†HTML (experimental)】\nL38:   * 【14†TeX Source】\nL39: \nL40: 【15†view license†creativecommons.org】\nL41: \nL42: ### Additional Features\nL43: \nL44:   * 【16†Audio Summary】\nL45: ### Current browse context:\nL46: \nL47: cs.CR\nL48: \nL49: 【17†< prev】   |   【18†next >】\nL50: \nL51: 【19†new】 | 【20†recent】 | 【21†2024-02】\nL52: \nL53: Change to browse by:\nL5